&lt;/template&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9798217287161.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Dungeon Crawler Carl&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781939130204.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 6&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-na.ssl-images-amazon.com/images/P/1736576208.01._SCLZZZZZZZ_.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;How to Make a Video Game All By Yourself&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781939130679.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 7&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781939130686.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 8&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781941220153.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 9&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9780593949245.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;1929&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781941220160.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 10&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781941220467.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 11&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781941220474.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 12&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781506752921.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Cyberpunk&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781506726359.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Dawnrunner&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/71tPFknfgaL._SL1500_.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Epidemics of Modern Life&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9780593713402.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Playful&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421590554.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 1&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421592602.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 2&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421592619.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 3&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781785789038.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Written&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781607748892.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Story Genius&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9780593820278.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Carl&apos;s Doomsday Scenario&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781594205217.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;The Genius of Birds&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781982149444.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;How to Write a Mystery&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9780063375833.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Murder on the Orient Express&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781974746910.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Hirayasumi Vol. 1&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781974746927.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Hirayasumi Vol. 2&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781974749249.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Hirayasumi, Vol. 3&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421593029.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 4&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421593036.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 5&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781974751822.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Hirayasumi, Vol. 4&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421593043.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 6&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421595054.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 7&quot;&gt;&lt;img loading=&quot;lazy&quot; src=&quot;https://images-us.bookshop.org/ingram/9781421595061.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 8&quot;&gt;&lt;/mini-bookshelf&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3U1gfOP&quot;&gt;Dungeon Crawler Carl&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This was my going to bed book, so I read it one page at a time and it took me over a year to finish. Despite putting me to sleep every night, it’s a fun little snack of a book and I see why it’s having a moment. I will confess tho, the first half of the book felt like grinding through a tutorial level and I almost gave up. But glad I stuck with it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3QufjnG&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 6&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The prequel continues as more and more people are pulled into the war’s grasp. A lot of space drama and the series is really hitting its stride here.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/42Lqj2R&quot;&gt;How to Make a Video Game All By Yourself&lt;/a&gt; ★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;A short little inspo book. I enjoyed it but not a lot of tactical insights beyond “Use a game engine” and “Just do it.” Maybe could have been a blog post.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4eQinod&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 7&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The final set of stories from the prequel to the original series. Might be my favorite. It catalogs the horrifying start of the Gundam Universe’s central plot line, The One Year War. It’s here you understand that Gundam is not about space robots (although it is very much about that), but a story about people who find themselves in a gruesome war.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/43maf7K&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 8&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;We return to the main story timeline with tons of intense combat scenes that are beautifully illustrated and inked. You feel the drum beats of war pushing towards a climatic end of the series.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4f1ndiG&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 9&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★½&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The tension is building towards the final battle. Based on the title of this edition I thought it would cover more of Lalah’s story arc, but she’s really a minor character… yet her need to be protected shows her importance.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3RnjpOQ&quot;&gt;1929&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This book raises a few hackles. It is frustrating that names and institutions found in this book who wrought the greatest economic crash in history –names like Carnegie, Lamont, Mellon,Durant, and JP Morgan– are still household names today. It’s doubly frustrating that this seems to be the current Wall Street/AI/Crypto playbook; speculate and wash trade, but they’re “smarter” this time and have the FDIC backstop to prevent bank runs.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4tUqWlE&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 10&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4dDOaq3&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 11&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3PZDCK8&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam: The Origin, Vol. 12&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The ending of Gundam The: Origin over the last three novels was perfect. Epic Space Battles. Deception. Betrayal. Death. Revolution. Vindication. It has everything and I cannot recommend this series enough if you get the opportunity to read it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3RFJnx7&quot;&gt;Cyberpunk&lt;/a&gt; ★★★½&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;A fun comic, but maybe not my cup of tea. It might be a translation issue. I found the writing very… Americanized. I would probably benefit from watching the Netflix series that picks up later in the chronology or playing the video and/or TTRPG.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4fQJRue&quot;&gt;Dawnrunner&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Humans piloting big robots. Pacific Rim with a little bit of Evangelion. Great art style.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/453143/oliver-burkeman-epidemics-of-modern-life-by-burkeman-oliver/9781529198010&quot;&gt;Epidemics of Modern Life&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Technically not a book but a collection of radio broadcasts by one of my most enjoyable authors of late, Oliver Burkeman. Burkeman looks into four modern day phenomenon and offers his thoughts and counter-thoughts:&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Addicted to Busy: Why Life Has Got So Hectic&lt;/strong&gt; - Why do we say we’re busy? What happened to leisure being the height of wealth?&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Power of Negative Thinking&lt;/strong&gt; - The data on positive thinking is bad, negative thinking might be more effective.&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why Are We So Angry?&lt;/strong&gt; - Why do humans have anger? How have we genetically bred it? Can it be good?&lt;/li&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Death of Nuance&lt;/strong&gt; - What do we lose when we eliminate nuance?&lt;/li&gt; &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;p&gt;Alls to say, this was a series I needed right now in my life. I found it very meditative and refreshing. Like a cool breeze.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4em7msS&quot;&gt;Playful&lt;/a&gt; DNF&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;I love the idea of play as a human right. It’s how we learn. It’s how we adapt. It’s how we explore boundaries. But I got to the part where it reinforced one of the dumbest parenting tropes: “Kids these days! iPads! All I needed as a kid was cardboard boxes.” At that point I nope’d out of the book.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;I loved cardboard growing up but I also played a fuckton of Nintendo. I gave my kids plenty of cardboard-itunities and it took me awhile to realize they don’t create like that. My son wants to manage things. My daughter wants to get 10,000 gems and fingernails and spread them out on every available surface of my house. Kids play differently. Adults do too.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4aJHnuf&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 1&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Gundam… but let’s give all the robots bigger guns, more shields, and enormous backpacks with jet boosters. This series really drives the “War is Hell” theme of the Gundam Universe.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4vxC1ej&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 2&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4uSzYjx&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 3&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;I’m enjoying this series but this was some of the best space mecha battle art I’ve ever seen.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4g1rGlO&quot;&gt;Written&lt;/a&gt; ★★★½&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Good advice from people who understand how writers get blocked. Their advice is basic but comes down to establishing a writing practice and finding out where you’re successful writing. The tactical portion devolves into setting SMART goals which is mid-tier advice to me personally but I think helpful in the context and generally a good starting place for people with no productivity framework at all.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4waH8AU&quot;&gt;Story Genius&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Focus on the main character. Give every scene a purpose. A really helpful and usable framework for organizing the chaos in my brain.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4ffAZO5&quot;&gt;Carl&apos;s Doomsday Scenario&lt;/a&gt; &lt;small&gt;[In progress]&lt;/small&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4ePdEkO&quot;&gt;The Genius of Birds&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;I learned a lot from this book. Birds are wicked smart. It maybe even radicalized me a bit against capitalism. That’s a longer story for another time. But birds are cool.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4vGCWIA&quot;&gt;How to Write a Mystery&lt;/a&gt; DNF&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Pretty great… but with over seventy contributions it got a little repetitive really quick. I may come back to this or pick it up again because there were quite a few gems of wisdom in there. But I was fine to put it down.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4bzhP3w&quot;&gt;Murder on the Orient Express&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★½&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;After listening to most of &lt;em&gt;How to Write a Mystery&lt;/em&gt;, I felt compelled to finally pickup one of the greatest mystery novels ever written. Not that you need my opinion, but it lives up to the hype. The audiobook version had an extra special flare with quite a few international accents being thrown around. Would recommend, even if Poirot’s accent is a bit too thick.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/45cewf1&quot;&gt;Hirayasumi Vol. 1&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is a charming series that really makes you rethink happiness.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/4gNZC5V&quot;&gt;Hirayasumi Vol. 2&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://link.amazon/B0hnRPMqg&quot;&gt;Hirayasumi, Vol. 3&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://link.amazon/B01GeldlE&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 4&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;The character art on this one was noticeably… bad? Thunderbolt character art overall is very Uncanny Valley with spiky-haired edgelord-iness framing soft faces, but the thick lines in this volume were distinctly odd. Good story tho.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://link.amazon/B0atV8HCv&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 5&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/45jcWbr&quot;&gt;Hirayasumi, Vol. 4&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/3RCj4sc&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 6&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;div style=&quot;font-size: 0.925rem&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;This volume does a neat little bit of reverse chronological storytelling.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/45i6a5H&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 7&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/45jd76B&quot;&gt;Mobile Suit Gundam Thunderbolt, Vol. 8&lt;/a&gt; ★★★★★&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;blogging&quot;&gt;📝 &lt;strong&gt;Blogging&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;#blogging&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;📝 Blogging&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;📝 Blogging&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A wretched season for blogging. There’s a bonafide CSS trick in there, so that’s nice. But most of my free time went to scooping rocks out of my yard (see above) and reading space mech manga (see above).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;/2026/06/spoons-by-sam/&quot;&gt;Spoons by Sam&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;/2026/06/fix-for-host-has-slot/&quot;&gt;A workaround for hiding empty slots in Chromium&lt;/a&gt;   &lt;span class=&quot;tag&quot;&gt;#CSS&lt;/span&gt;,   &lt;span class=&quot;tag&quot;&gt;#webcomponents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;/2026/06/curated-public-domain-images/&quot;&gt;Finding curated public domain images&lt;/a&gt;   &lt;span class=&quot;tag&quot;&gt;#blogging&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;/2026/07/codepen-2/&quot;&gt;The new yet familiar CodePen 2.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;/2026/07/number-chart-go-up/&quot;&gt;Before I go: Double your business with companies you like&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I think a lot about writing – even listened to three different books about writing (see above) – but a lot of my writing brain waves go towards thinking about the fiction I want to write.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;media-diet&quot;&gt;📺 Media Diet&lt;a href=&quot;#media-diet&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;📺 Media Diet&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;📺 Media Diet&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Movies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I made a significant dent in my watchlist this summer thanks to travel and some lazy weekends.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/bugonia/&quot;&gt;Bugonia&lt;/a&gt; (2025) - A story about people giving into their conspiracy theories. Emma Stone is incredible in the film.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/micro-budget/&quot;&gt;Micro Budget&lt;/a&gt; (2024) - A mockumentary about a low-budget film gone awry. It felt like an elongated So I Think You Should Leave cringe-sketch, but worse I guess.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/the-breadwinner-2026/&quot;&gt;The Breadwinner&lt;/a&gt; (2026) - I like Nate Bargatze, this was an okay movie.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/the-sheep-detectives/&quot;&gt;The Sheep Detectives&lt;/a&gt; (2026) - A kids movie where talking sheep solve a murder mystery. Pretty good.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/hoppers/&quot;&gt;Hoppers&lt;/a&gt; (2026) - Animated film with an ecological twist. I liked it but one bias I have is that Bobby Moynihan voices a major character and that’s enough for me to love it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/the-fifth-element/&quot;&gt;The Fifth Element&lt;/a&gt; (1997) - This has been on my list of shame for a long time. But I was pretty Christian in 1997 and this movie famously has a naked lady in it so I steered clear. Alas, it’s a great movie. It has everything you could want from aliens, to big guns, to practical effects. My biggest takeaway though was that this movie was perfectly cast. Every character is perfect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/anaconda-2025/&quot;&gt;Anaconda&lt;/a&gt; (2025) - A decent comedy about Paul Rudd, Steve Zahn, and Jack Black attempting to make a shot for shot remake of the 1997 hit &lt;a href=&quot;https://letterboxd.com/film/anaconda/&quot;&gt;Anaconda&lt;/a&gt; starring Ice Cube and J. Lo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Beyond that, my son is catching up on the Marvel MCU so I’ve gotten the chance to watch some of the greatest hits with him.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TV&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bodkin (2025, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.netflix.com/title/81423482&quot;&gt;Netflix&lt;/a&gt;) - Will Forte is a murder podcast host investigating a mysterious disappearance in a small Irish town.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Game Changer S8 (2026, &lt;a href=&quot;https://watch.dropout.tv/game-changer/season:8&quot;&gt;Dropout&lt;/a&gt;) - This season is so good. This season is almost abstract art.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Welcome to Wrexham S5 (2026, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hulu.com/series/welcome-to-wrexham-c6906d50-d06c-40d1-a57c-1885d9dc2fef&quot;&gt;Hulu&lt;/a&gt;) - Up the town. A dramatic season for the boys from North Wales. I am a bit saddened by the fact they’ve nearly wholesaled the entire team… but they (Rob and Ryan) seem to share that sentiment to some degree.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Anime&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Violet Evergarden S1 (&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.netflix.com/title/80182123&quot;&gt;Netflix&lt;/a&gt;) - an anime about a military assassin robot, turned letter writer.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The Apothocary Diaries S1 (&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.netflix.com/title/81712068&quot;&gt;Netflix&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.crunchyroll.com/series/G3KHEVDJ7/the-apothecary-diaries&quot;&gt;CrunchyRoll&lt;/a&gt;)  - a mix of historical period drama and procedural medical drama, a young girl serves in the court of the emperor as a poison tester and investigator to mysterious happenings amongst the emperor’s stable of concubines and ladies in waiting. It’s like Shogun meets House MD… or something.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Podcasts&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I declared podcast bankruptcy and unsubscribed from all shows. I was feeling haunted by the unread counts so I’m taking a break. If you love something set it free.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;-recording&quot;&gt;🎙 Recording&lt;a href=&quot;#-recording&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;🎙 Recording&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;🎙 Recording&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;podcast-feed&gt;&lt;/podcast-feed&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;script&gt;
customElements.define(&apos;podcast-feed&apos;, class extends HTMLElement {
#items = [{ link: &apos;https://daverupert.com&apos;, title: { rendered: &apos;Hello world&apos; }}];

constructor() {
super();
this.renderItems();
}

get items() {
return this.#items
}

set items(data) {
this.#items = data;
this.renderItems();
}

renderItems() {
if(!this.#items) return;

const tmpl = `
&lt;ul&gt;
${this.#items.map((item) =&gt; {
return  `&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;${ item.link }&quot;&gt;${item.title.rendered}&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;`
}).join(&apos;&apos;)}
&lt;/ul&gt;
`
this.innerHTML = tmpl;
}
})

const feedUrl = &apos;https://shoptalkshow.com/wp-json/wp/v2/posts?_fields=title,link,date&amp;before=2026-08-10T05:23:00&amp;after=2026-05-05T13:41:00&amp;per_page=50&apos;;

fetch(feedUrl)
.then(res =&gt; res.json())
.then(json =&gt; 
document.querySelector(&apos;podcast-feed&apos;).items = json
).catch(err =&gt; console.log(err))
&lt;/script&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;hobbies&quot;&gt;🧶 Hobbies&lt;a href=&quot;#hobbies&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;🧶 Hobbies&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;🧶 Hobbies&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’ve been 3D printing a lot this summer. I went hard on printing organizers for my office, specifically embracing the Gridfinity system for organizing storage. I’m not “totally organized” yet but I completed &lt;em&gt;Project Reduce and Organize Cables&lt;/em&gt; and it feels great and there are a couple of prints that I’m still smitten by. It’s still neat and novel to summon objects out of thin air… and plastic.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;gunpla&quot;&gt;🤖 Gunpla&lt;a href=&quot;#gunpla&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;🤖 Gunpla&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;🤖 Gunpla&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’ve fallen off this horse but I’m feeling the draw to get back on since I’ve been reading tons of Gundam manga.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I bought some new acrylic paints to hopefully finish an experimental kit I embarked on but gave up on. I’m hoping once some other projects get cleared out I can sink back into gunpla again.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;️open-source&quot;&gt;⌨️ Open source&lt;a href=&quot;#️open-source&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;⌨️ Open source&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;⌨️ Open source&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A big summer for open source projects I work on but you wouldn’t know about it because I don’t talk about it enough:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://fast.design/&quot;&gt;FASTElement v3 is out&lt;/a&gt;! FASTElement in your bundle should be about 30% smaller&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://storybooks.fluentui.dev/web-components/&quot;&gt;Fluent Web Components v3 is out&lt;/a&gt;! After a two year beta, the web component version of Fluent is ready for public consumption. They’re already in use in across some Microsoft projects, so that feels pretty good.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Worked with &lt;a href=&quot;https://ericwbailey.design/&quot;&gt;Eric&lt;/a&gt; to add an &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/a11yproject/a11yproject.com/issues/1667&quot;&gt;AI Policy for The A11y Project&lt;/a&gt;. We built up a backlog of requests to have new apps and projects featured on the resources page, but most of them fly-by-night slop so we had to set some parameters around what we’re looking for when it comes to resources.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;-digital-gardening&quot;&gt;🌱 Digital Gardening&lt;a href=&quot;#-digital-gardening&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;🌱 Digital Gardening&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;🌱 Digital Gardening&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rolled out the new element-scoped view-transitions on my bookshelf. Most people probably won’t notice, but I feel the micro-difference and FPS boost and that’s what matters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Also on my stories page I rolled out a &lt;a href=&quot;https://leanrada.com/notes/css-only-lqip/&quot;&gt;CSS-only Mini LQIP trick&lt;/a&gt; that converts a 6-byte code into a blurry background-image placeholder to reduce CLS. Super happy with the effect.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Behind the scenes I improved my local Jekyll setup with the help of Copilot tackling tasks like upgrading to Ruby to v4, fixing some outdated gem issues, auditing my entire build pipeline, and removing some slower filters (e.g. &lt;code&gt;sassify&lt;/code&gt;). In general, it’s going well and I can build my entire site in 2.5s and 0.5s with archive templates disabled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I’ve had the pleasure in my career of sitting in tons of different board rooms talking with executives and getting a rundown on different charts and graphs they care most deeply about. As expected, “number chart go up” is always the desired outcome. Number chart go up is good because it makes other number charts go up, specifically stock ticker number.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;One common refrain I’d hear everywhere I went whether they dealt in pizzas, coffee, coupons, computers, or software licenses went something like this (paraphrasing), “On average customers use our product once per month and if we can double that number then we’d have no more problems.” An over-simplification of course, but they’re desperate to move the number from one… to two.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It doesn’t take a business degree to figure that out that doubling your sales, downloads, or clicks is good for business. But sometimes the goals are much more modest. Let’s say it’s coffee. They know they have you hooked on a drug and you’ll make at least four weekly purchases a month, but if they could get all their customers to buy a &lt;em&gt;fifth&lt;/em&gt; cup of coffee that month… that’s a measly five bucks for you and I the consumer, but that’s +20% revenue month-over-month for their store.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Putting this knowledge into the hands of consumers like us… Our five bucks feels meaningless and this feels like impotent advice on the purchasing power of the dollar, but I know it matters. Those five bucks can and will skew the averages and the people wearing Patagonia vests in mahogany-paneled board rooms will obsess over those charts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Collectively we can influence the number chart. It could be a coordinated buying campaign or boycott, but I think it could be as simple as re-patronizing small businesses you already enjoy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Double your purchases from that shop you liked, even if it’s a little too expensive because you understand they deal in small volumes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Go to that local restaurant you like again, the one where the owner comes out and shakes everyone’s hand.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Buy another online course with your company’s education budget from that online helpful person, even if you’ll never use it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Watch one more video, read one more post, like it, subscribe. And don’t block the ads.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And the inverse is also true…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make one less order from that big online retailer funneling dark money into our elections.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Cancel your subscription to companies owned by race-baiting ketamine-addled nazis.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Make one less post on the billionaire owned platform, delete the app, let your session expire.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And sometimes, you don’t even need to buy anything! Often, talking about the places you like has the biggest impact. Talking raises awareness, it puts good things and places in our collective consciousness. It won’t be instant, but hopefully we can reprogram our muscle memory to think of good company before giving money to bad company.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Bad companies pay a lot of ad-bucks to other bad companies to get into your field of view. Ads are why websites have gone to shit. It’s why Instagram has become unusable, not to mention that it’s become a giant multi-level marketing scam where it’s all influencers hustling their dropshipped garbage to each other instead of sharing cool photos with friends.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Become a free, one-off marketing department or brand ambassador for a restaurant, app, podcast, artist, game, band, or store that you think is good. No one is going to think “Damn, Dave keeps talking about these breakfast tacos at &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.dans-hamburgers.com/&quot;&gt;Dan’s Hamburgers&lt;/a&gt; in Austin, TX that are only available before 10:30 am, they must be complete shit.” They will be curious and figure it out for themselves and their lives will be forever changed until the day Dan’s gets swallowed up by Austin’s endless “progress”.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the wake of a phalanx of tech billionaires shit-grinning behind an orange-hued fascist criminal who four years prior incited a coup in that same rotunda, some Europeans have began advocating for local technology solutions. And even though &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.switch-to.eu/en&quot;&gt;these&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://european-alternatives.eu/&quot;&gt;European&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://builtineu.eu/&quot;&gt;alternative&lt;/a&gt; sites post a slight existential threat to my family’s income, I love the initiative. I wish every continent, country, city, and state had similar efforts to drive money towards locally sourced alternatives. I’m guilty of it as well, but why we funnel so much money to five nearly identical technology companies boggles the mind.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anyways. Consider doubling your business somewhere with. some place &lt;em&gt;or someone&lt;/em&gt; that deserves it. And the smaller that business is, the even bigger impact you’ll have on the number chart.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CodePen v2 is now released! Because &lt;a href=&quot;https://shoptalkshow.com/&quot;&gt;I co-host a podcast with Chris&lt;/a&gt; I’ve had an up-close perspective at this monumental effort over the last five years. I know how much work has gone into this release and I applaud the team for hitting the finish line.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At the crux of the new experience is the new and improved editor. When you first launch into the v2.0 editor, your first response will probably be “Who moved my cheese!?” New is always uncomfortable, but there’s a fix for getting back to the old familiar UI…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hit &lt;code&gt;⌘ + U&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;CTRL + U&lt;/code&gt; to hide the new pen sidebar&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Install the &lt;a href=&quot;https://codepen.io/editor/team/CodePenTemplates/pen/0198ce76-3d55-76ae-8cf7-edf6860caa95&quot;&gt;Classic Block Template&lt;/a&gt; to remove ~14 lines of HTML (Optional)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Okay, now hit &lt;code&gt;⌘ + U&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;CTRL + U&lt;/code&gt; again, because what’s in the sidebar are all the new features and favorite parts I want to talk about.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;an-entire-file-system&quot;&gt;An entire file system&lt;a href=&quot;#an-entire-file-system&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;An entire file system&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;An entire file system&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src=&quot;https://cdn.daverupert.com/posts/2026/slaps-roof-of-codepen.jpg&quot; alt=&quot;slaps roof of car meme: slaps roof of codepen, this bad boy can fit over 300 files&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The first thing you’ll notice when entering the new CodePen editor is a file explorer. That’s right! CodePen is breaking out of its three-file constraint and allowing you to build entire multi-file and multi-page websites.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A not-so-secret feature of CodePen was that you could mimic a file system in CodePen by &lt;a href=&quot;https://blog.codepen.io/documentation/adding-external-resources/#you-can-use-other-pens-as-external-resources&quot;&gt;using other pens as external resources&lt;/a&gt;, but referencing your custom &lt;code&gt;reset.css&lt;/code&gt; by importing &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;user&amp;gt;/pen/&amp;lt;slug&amp;gt;.css&lt;/code&gt; wasn’t the JS module-like experience you come to expect from the modern web.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;But gone are the days of 2,000 line JavaScript files, I can now &lt;code&gt;import foo from &apos;./foo.js&apos;&lt;/code&gt; like TC39 intended. I must admit that my more complex ideas were hitting the limit of what was comfortable to type into a tiny textbox. But now creating a folder of web components or JSON data files to loop through is easier than ever. I can build multi-file and multi-page prototypes (e.g. view-transitions! service-workers!) all without spinning up a local dev server. Digging up an old CodePen is much easier than finding an old folder on an old hard drive and it’s arguably the easiest way to put a custom webpage up on the internet right now.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Without drawing too many comparisons to competitors, CodePen at times felt a bit underpowered when compared to other online code editing environments. But those other services that spin up an entire Node.js Docker so that I can build my &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;fart-button&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; always felt like overkill to me. CodePen now occupies not just the entry-level and middle ground, but almost the entire spectrum of needs that I have for prototyping and small site building.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;versioning-and-deployments&quot;&gt;Versioning and deployments&lt;a href=&quot;#versioning-and-deployments&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;Versioning and deployments&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;Versioning and deployments&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Who amongst us hasn’t totally beefer’d up a good pen before?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ruining an otherwise quality pen to idea creep or losing work to a browser refresh is always a possibility with online editors. It’s typically not world-ending for me and my …&lt;em&gt;ahem&lt;/em&gt;… spartanly styled pens, but for sure results in a couple choice swear words. I imagine &lt;a href=&quot;https://codepen.io/trending&quot;&gt;trending tab experts&lt;/a&gt; and newbie beginners find this even more frustrating. But now pens have a version history so you can roll back if necessary.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The deployments are better now too. Sometimes when building an idea, I like to send the “chromeless” version of the pen to non-developer friends or partners/clients to avoid overwhelming them with CodePen UI. This is also useful when debugging on your phone. The &lt;code&gt;debug/&lt;/code&gt;URL worked, but if you’ve ever URL-hacked a private pen URL on your phone you know how un-fun that is. But now I can deploy the pen, get a nice &lt;code&gt;codepen.dev&lt;/code&gt; URL or point my custom subdomain at CodePen’s DNS, and Airdop that URL to myself. A small but meaningful quality of life improvement for me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;blocks&quot;&gt;Blocks&lt;a href=&quot;#blocks&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;Blocks&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;Blocks&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My favorite feature of the new CodePen is Blocks. One of my biggest gripes with modern web development is the amount of tooling it takes to get started. We’ve made it so damn complex to make a webpage, no wonder AI is so popular.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Blocks adds a visual layer to the opaque world of preprocessors, linters, and build processes. CodePen takes those processes and stack them on top of each other to form a predictable pipeline that you can easily visualize and understand how the sausage gets made. Imagine how this must feel for beginners compared to a dozen config files littering the root of your repo! Web development demystified.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’ve wanted a UI like this for build processes for a long time: Gulp, Webpack, Vite, GitHub Actions, Azure DevOps Pipelines… I feel every service would be better if it had a Blocks UI. Like &lt;a href=&quot;https://scratch.mit.edu/&quot;&gt;Scratch&lt;/a&gt;, but for servers. Experienced developers might even be less afraid to operate on their build tools if they had something like this.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I think CodePen has created something special here that most people won’t fully understand. A perfect fit for their beginner audience as well as their professional audience who don’t want to copy, paste, and manage config files to build a simple website or goofy pen.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-simplified-but-more-powerful-ui&quot;&gt;A simplified but more powerful UI&lt;a href=&quot;#a-simplified-but-more-powerful-ui&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;A simplified but more powerful UI&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;A simplified but more powerful UI&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A whole new product with a new file-system, versioned deployments, and customizable build chain smooshed in… yet the UI still feels familiar. It’s rare for products to evolve so intuitively. There’s more features, but everything feels more predictable. In the “classic” CodePen editor you’d have to surf through a handful of dropdowns and modals to tweak your settings and now a lot of that work is automatic. You want Sass? Add the &lt;code&gt;.scss&lt;/code&gt; file extension. You want TypeScript? Add the &lt;code&gt;.ts&lt;/code&gt; extension. CodePen will add all the Blocks for you with a default config that works.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It feels like all the configuration options are all co-located in the right place now. And the new omnibar search box is the ultimate get-out-of-jail-free card, exposing most of the features you’d need without all the hunting and pecking. Expanding the capabilities of the product while maintaining simplicity deserves some applause and I feel the team at CodePen walked this delicate line and succeeded.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-couple-final-thoughts&quot;&gt;A couple final thoughts&lt;a href=&quot;#a-couple-final-thoughts&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;A couple final thoughts&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;A couple final thoughts&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I’ve said it before (and I’ll say it again) I admire the team at CodePen and –despite my obvious biases– I think they’re one of the best product teams out there. &lt;a href=&quot;https://blog.codepen.io/2026/07/23/435-the-2-0-launch-team-codepens-favorite-features/&quot;&gt;In their podcast&lt;/a&gt; they give you a rare, honest, behind-the-scenes look at how they are thinking about and building their product. No one even comes close to their level of transparency.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Probably my favorite part of Team CodePen is that they’re not afraid to tackle the hard work and pay down technical debt when needed. They stay small, sweat the details, and take their time. There’s no back-pressure from venture capital to move fast and break your things. In my view it’s software done right.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because I was part of the beta, I’m already using the hell out of CodePen 2.0. I’ve built dozens of big ideas (a browser-based camera, a service-worker based declarative shadow DOM solution, a half-dozen in-browser AI chatbots, a minimum viable design system, and more) that would have been either cumbersome or next-to-impossible in the previous editor. CodePen 2.0 is already enabling me to build bigger, better, and more complex ideas without leaving the comfort of one of my favorite applications on the whole entire internet.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So thanks, CodePen, for the huge upgrade and congrats to the team on the launch. 🎉&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;figure&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;https://cdn.daverupert.com/posts/2026/book-of-discoveries.jpg?optimize&quot; width=&quot;1200&quot; height=&quot;599&quot; alt=&quot;a vintage illustration of a man sitting on a chair in a sparse room holding up a book that appears illuminated. a small dog in the foreground stares at his owner and the book.&quot;&gt;
&lt;figcaption&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Xavier de Maistre in an armchair, displaying his “book of discoveries” (Veyssier, &lt;a href=&quot;https://archive.org/details/journeyroundmyro00mais/page/n17/mode/2up&quot;&gt;1860&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Every so often I like to include a public domain image in a blog post. I’m fond of the woodblock and lithographic illustrations of the late 1800s. I try to avoid indiscriminately (un-)splashing every post but the real reason for the infrequency isn’t radical self-control, it’s because dumpster diving for public domain images can be a chore.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;You can –of course– use Google or Bing to find public domain images but the volume, duplicates, and weighing the veracity of copyright attribution is overwhelming. The Library of Congress has &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.loc.gov/free-to-use/&quot;&gt;Free to Use and Reuse sets&lt;/a&gt;, but their collections are nebulous noun buckets filled with a federally-mandated amount of Americana. On top of that, searching library sites is never a good experience to me. They tend to be slow and inaccurate with poor quality results and it’s a matter of time until you’re redirected to a smaller local library collection or database that’s even less maintained.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That’s why –for me– a little for-profit curation goes a long way. I wrote down some of my favorite resources from my last search (so I can remember next time) and will try to keep a running list of good resources I find.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://publicdomainreview.org/&quot;&gt;The Public Domain Review&lt;/a&gt; writes blog posts about interesting works and curates quality finds in the &lt;a href=&quot;https://pdimagearchive.org/galleries/all/random/desc&quot;&gt;Public Domain Image Gallery&lt;/a&gt;. Notably, The PBR has a cool “&lt;a href=&quot;https://pdimagearchive.org/infinite-view/&quot;&gt;Infinite View&lt;/a&gt;” way to browse. It’s a bit overwhelming for me, but you may love it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://artvee.com/&quot;&gt;Artvee&lt;/a&gt; is a site with some great curation of public domain images.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.heritagetype.com/&quot;&gt;Heritage Type&lt;/a&gt; maintains some nice &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.heritagetype.com/pages/free-vintage-illustrations&quot;&gt;collections of vintage illustrations&lt;/a&gt; , almost like tattoo flash art in PNG and vector formats.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The business models are similar; pair the public domain with something to sell. The Public Domain Review and Artvee sell prints, aprons, or coffee mugs of the public domain works while Heritage Type funnels you to their quality fonts and design assets. I know we all hate Capitalism, but taking a small cut off the top for quality curation seems like a good trade and not an abuse of the commons.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Blessed are the curators.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Named slots are one of web components’ biggest &lt;a href=&quot;https://htmlwithsuperpowers.netlify.app/&quot;&gt;superpowers ✨&lt;/a&gt;. Imagine a Button component with an optional icon; in Web Components we don’t need a separate Button and IconButton, a single Button component with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;slot name=&amp;quot;icon&amp;quot;&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; will do. Or a card component with a handful of predefined slots for image, title, description, price, metadata, etc. Not 100 different cards… one card.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slots are a wonderful way to declaratively compose UI, but one shadow styling challenge that has vexed my team over the years is how to contextually hide a &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;slot&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; when there’s no slotted content.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-problem-ghost-gaps&quot;&gt;The problem… ghost gaps&lt;a href=&quot;#the-problem-ghost-gaps&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;The problem… ghost gaps&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;The problem… ghost gaps&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The most idiomatic way to check if a particular slot is in-use is to use &lt;code&gt;:host(:has([slot=&amp;quot;foo&amp;quot;]))&lt;/code&gt;. But… In a weird twist to how web component browser-compat bugs tend to go, &lt;code&gt;:host(:has([slot]))&lt;/code&gt; works in Safari and Firefox today but doesn’t work in Chromium!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Chromium not supporting &lt;code&gt;:host(:has([slot]))&lt;/code&gt; means we can’t query an element’s Light DOM contents from the Shadow DOM. That interpretation of encapsulation makes sense (I guess) and in most situations it’s not a big problem; an empty &lt;code&gt;slot&lt;/code&gt; is like an empty &lt;code&gt;span&lt;/code&gt; and probably won’t impact rendering. But it becomes a problem when the empty slots appear in a &lt;code&gt;flex&lt;/code&gt; or &lt;code&gt;grid&lt;/code&gt; layout using &lt;code&gt;gap&lt;/code&gt; because unless you hide the element and/or its wrapper, the gap will not collapse in Chromium, so you end up with a “ghost gap”.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: With &lt;code&gt;slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; content in Light DOM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;optional-slot-demo-broken&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;required&quot;&gt;Required content&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;optional&quot;&gt;Optional content - expected: show content with gap&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/optional-slot-demo-broken&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;optional-slot-demo-broken&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;required&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Required content&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Optional content - expected: show content with gap&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/optional-slot-demo-broken&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: Without &lt;code&gt;slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; content in Light DOM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;optional-slot-demo-broken&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;required&quot;&gt;Required content&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/optional-slot-demo-broken&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;optional-slot-demo-broken&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;required&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Required content&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;!-- expected: does not show extra whitespace --&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/optional-slot-demo-broken&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Works in Safari = wrapper div hidden, no gap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Works in Firefox = wrapper div hidden, no gap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;❌ Broken in Chromium = extra whitespace below &lt;code&gt;slot=&amp;quot;required&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-failed-attempts&quot;&gt;The failed attempts…&lt;a href=&quot;#the-failed-attempts&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;The failed attempts…&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;The failed attempts…&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We’ve tried a lot of fixes over the last couple years. Variations on &lt;code&gt;:host(:has())&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;element-name:has()&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;!important&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;::slotted([slot]) ~ [slot]&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;::slotted([slot]:empty)&lt;/code&gt;, and all permutations of those. Copilot/Claude were certain these would work… but alas…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-fix-use-scope&quot;&gt;The fix… use @scope&lt;a href=&quot;#the-fix-use-scope&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;The fix… use @scope&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;The fix… use @scope&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This week my co-worker &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.linkedin.com/in/jeff-smith-28624711/&quot;&gt;Jeff&lt;/a&gt; and I were navigating this issue again. Coincidentally, the ShopTalkShow Discord was discussing the same exact problem and cited the &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/shoelace-style/shoelace/blob/next/src/internal/slot.ts&quot;&gt;&lt;code&gt;HasSlotController&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/a&gt; in &lt;a href=&quot;https://webawesome.com/&quot;&gt;WebAwesome&lt;/a&gt; (née Shoelace) as a working solution to this problem. I’d prefer to keep the solution in pure CSS, but was getting desperate.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While glancing through the code I noticed the use of &lt;code&gt;:scope&lt;/code&gt; in &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/shoelace-style/shoelace/blob/next/src/internal/slot.ts#L38-L40&quot;&gt;L38-40&lt;/a&gt; and while unrelated it sparked a curiosity to try to use &lt;code&gt;:scope&lt;/code&gt; to juke Chromium’s shadow limitations. A handful of tries later and I had a working solution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here’s what I came up with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-css&quot;&gt;:host {
display: grid;
gap: 1rem;
}

/* Hide optional slots by default */
.optional-slot {
display: none;
}

/* Show in Safari/Firefox */
:host(:has([slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;])) .optional-slot {
display: block;
}

/* Fix for Chromium */
@scope {
:scope:has([slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;]) .optional-slot {
display: block;
}
}
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: With &lt;code&gt;slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; content in Light DOM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;optional-slot-demo&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;required&quot;&gt;Required content&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;optional&quot;&gt;Optional content - expected: show content with gap&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/optional-slot-demo&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;optional-slot-demo&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;required&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Required content&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Optional content - expected: show content with gap&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/optional-slot-demo&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example: Without &lt;code&gt;slot=&amp;quot;optional&amp;quot;&lt;/code&gt; content in Light DOM&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;optional-slot-demo&gt;
&lt;div slot=&quot;required&quot;&gt;Required content&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/optional-slot-demo&gt;
&lt;pre&gt;&lt;code class=&quot;language-html&quot;&gt;&amp;lt;optional-slot-demo&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;div slot=&amp;quot;required&amp;quot;&amp;gt;Required content&amp;lt;/div&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;!-- expected: does not show extra whitespace --&amp;gt;
&amp;lt;/optional-slot-demo&amp;gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And the results…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Works in Safari = wrapper div hidden, no gap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Works in Firefox = wrapper div hidden, no gap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;✅ Works in Chrome = wrapper div hidden, no gap&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;the-why&quot;&gt;The why…&lt;a href=&quot;#the-why&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;The why…&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;The why…&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I love a good CSS-trick, but &lt;em&gt;even worse than a bug is not knowing why something works&lt;/em&gt;. My other teammate &lt;a href=&quot;https://marchbox.com/&quot;&gt;Zacky&lt;/a&gt; dug into the spec and confirmed this is a viable fix because &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-6/#cssscoperule&quot;&gt;as its defined&lt;/a&gt; &lt;code&gt;@scope&lt;/code&gt; without a scope-root in the shadow styles allows &lt;code&gt;:scope&lt;/code&gt; to match the shadow-host instead of the shadow-root… which is clutch. From the spec:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors-4/#scope-pseudo&quot;&gt;:scope&lt;/a&gt; selector can match the &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors-4/#featureless&quot;&gt;featureless&lt;/a&gt; shadow host when that host is the &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/selectors-4/#scoping-root&quot;&gt;scoping root&lt;/a&gt; element. (&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/9025&quot;&gt;Issue 9025&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And…&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-6/#at-ruledef-scope&quot;&gt;@scope&lt;/a&gt; rule without &lt;a href=&quot;https://drafts.csswg.org/css-cascade-6/#typedef-scope-start&quot;&gt;scope-start&lt;/a&gt; scopes to the shadow host instead of the &lt;a href=&quot;https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-shadow-root&quot;&gt;shadow root&lt;/a&gt;. (&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/9178&quot;&gt;Issue 9178&lt;/a&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks, Team CSSScopeRule, for enabling this workaround (&lt;em&gt;checks notes&lt;/em&gt;) three years ago. There’s a wonderful bit of irony that I’m using &lt;code&gt;@scope&lt;/code&gt; to break out of the Shadow DOM’s style containment and that’s part of why I love CSS.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I look forward to the day we can use &lt;code&gt;:host(:has())&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;:has-slotted()&lt;/code&gt; in all browsers, but in the meantime I’m happy to have stumbled upon a duplicative-yet-simple fix. Let me know if you end up using this and it works for you. I already know we have a handful of minor spacing bugs to patch up in our system.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I play &lt;a href=&quot;https://cluesbysam.com/&quot;&gt;Clues by Sam&lt;/a&gt; nearly every single day. &lt;a href=&quot;https://daverupert.com/2025/11/clues-by-sam/&quot;&gt;I wrote about it before&lt;/a&gt;, but solving a little crime puzzle adds a micro-thrill to my day. A little blast of logic, misdirection, and wordplay. It’s a frequent topic of conversation in the ShopTalkShow Discord where people share their solves like Wordle scores.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;figure&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;https://cdn.daverupert.com/posts/2026/lanarchiste.jpg?optimize=true&quot; alt=&quot;Vintage illustration of two square-ish police men approaching a crowd of people who have grabbed a wanted man.&quot; width=&quot;1070&quot; height=&quot;800&quot;&gt;
&lt;figcaption&gt;L&apos;anarchiste (1892) by Félix Vallotton. Public Domain.&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;It might have been &lt;a href=&quot;https://joshcollinsworth.com/&quot;&gt;Josh&lt;/a&gt; who first pointed it out, but as a group we’ve realized that &lt;em&gt;Clues by Sam&lt;/em&gt; is an uncanny proxy for measuring your emotional well-being that day. If you’re a regular puzzler, I think it’s safe to say you should be able to solve a non-weekend edition of &lt;em&gt;Clues by Sam&lt;/em&gt; without any mistakes (green days). But there are off-days (yellow days) where I make obvious errors tracking the four or five clues you need to solve the puzzle. And there are worse days (orange days) where even with hints, I have no idea what I’m doing and begin to doubt my understanding of English.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Borrowing the metaphor of &lt;a href=&quot;https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spoon_theory&quot;&gt;Spoon Theory&lt;/a&gt; from the disabled community, &lt;em&gt;Clues by Sam&lt;/em&gt; helps me take inventory of what’s left in my kitchen drawers. My performance on &lt;em&gt;Clues by Sam&lt;/em&gt; often alerts me to events happening in my subconscious. It’s become a barometer for my cognitive bandwidth; like Person, Man, Woman, Camera, TV but with Xena, Kyle, Paula, Vince, and Chloe.  Faceplanting on an easy puzzle is a &lt;em&gt;clue for me&lt;/em&gt; that I have some unarticulated anxieties occupying my mind.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It’s not rocket science that cognitive tasks are more difficult when you’re distracted, but the reliability to which this daily puzzler causes me to dig into my personal well-being is shocking. I’m beginning to see obvious mistakes –like the common “crimocent” mix-up– less as a failure and more as an opportunity to pause and assess what else might be going on in my life.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Forgive me, Reader. It’s been five months since my last vibe check. That’s a lot of ground to cover and it’s not possible to get into everything that happened. Like in real life conversations, instead of telling you &lt;em&gt;how&lt;/em&gt; I’m doing, I’ll tell you &lt;em&gt;what&lt;/em&gt; I’ve been doing these past few months.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-trip-to-seattle&quot;&gt;A trip to Seattle&lt;a href=&quot;#a-trip-to-seattle&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;A trip to Seattle&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;A trip to Seattle&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;figure&gt;
&lt;img src=&quot;https://cdn.daverupert.com/posts/2026/microsoft.jpg&quot; width=&quot;1200&quot; height=&quot;700&quot; alt=&quot;a large outdoor sign of the microsoft logo on the microsoft campus&quot;&gt;
&lt;figcaption&gt;An underwhelming, low-res photo I took on my Kodak Charmera&lt;/figcaption&gt;
&lt;/figure&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In March I went to Redmond, WA for “Meetup Week” at work. This was my second visit to the mothership in two years of working at Microsoft. Ideally, I’d go up more… but not too much… so this is fine. We’re a high-functioning remote team even though nearly everyone on the team has been RTO’d.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Speaking of my team, the people I get to work with are super great. Talented, friendly people I’m lucky to work with. We’re a scrappy bunch punching above our weight.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The people in my broader design studio are also great. I spent a good chunk of my time helping folks with their vibe-coding setups. Ever since the mandate dropped earlier this year, a lot of my job has been helping to onboard our ~200 new junior &lt;del&gt;designers&lt;/del&gt; &lt;em&gt;developers&lt;/em&gt; and their agent buddies to developer tools. I will say… it’s real easy to get your computer into a borked state when installing a lot of new tools. Chatbots and emergent AI tools make it worse. Getting comfortable with the terminal was a multi-year process for me and we’re asking “normies” to hot-drop in and approve hundreds of minified bash commands per day. I feel for everyone who is having their boat rocked by all the change.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Meetings and roadmap discussions filled my week. We had a couple memorable team dinners and bar conversations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My week was super busy but I was able to sneak a couple moments to catch up with some old friends and colleagues in the Puget Sound area. Spent an afternoon with Adam, shared a beer with Kyle, and talked accessibility with Aaron. Good times.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My flight back to Austin got caught in a minor snowpocalypse. My 6pm flight got delayed until midnight which means I didn’t get home until 6:30am. Woof. And my seatmate next to me on the plane –the one with the crinkly bag of food who liked to have full volume conversations with her husband in the seat in front of her– was… &lt;em&gt;ahem&lt;/em&gt;… less sensitive to the lack-of-sleep situation we were all in. Oh well.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;a-trip-to-tampa&quot;&gt;A trip to Tampa&lt;a href=&quot;#a-trip-to-tampa&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;A trip to Tampa&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;A trip to Tampa&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;My daughter’s cheer team made it to the big end-of-season competition in Tampa, FL. We took the whole family and turned it into a mini-vacation. Her team did great and “hit zero” (zero deductions) but so did eight other teams in her division and only seven advanced. I’m proud of my little athlete and she continues to amaze me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While not in the thundering jock-jam confines of the civic convention center, we took a trip to the beach, saw some movies, and went to Universal Studios Orlando. We aren’t a super theme park family but I was feeling a bit of parental guilt that my kids would be telling their future therapists about how we never took them to theme parks. Knowing my kids don’t love rollercoasters or waiting in lines, we stuck to the main park which has rides for the Minions, Diagon Alley, and the Simpsons. I splurged (read: paid double) for the “express pass” so we could skip the lines whenever possible.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Diagon Alley was the biggest highlight. That comes with some regret because JK Rowling sucks now. But the experience they crafted there is so meticulously well done you can’t help but feel immersed. I admired all the theme park tricks they do to make everything seem bigger. Slanted walls to make it feel fantastical and disorient you combined with how the windows get progressively smaller on the upper stories of the buildings so they seem taller. I spent a good amount of time marvelling at a set of stairs that went to nowhere; there to create an illusion that the town keeps going on forever. I love those details. Then on top of that they add in these immersive wand experience gimmicks (we didn’t buy wands but watched a others cast incantations). Very thoughtful experience design and that’s what I think I liked about it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I will say that 4D experience rides are not for me. The “DO NOT RIDE IF…” warnings they give at the beginning of each ride are basically a printout of my medical conditions. I know theme parks are doubling down on these because the cost per square foot is too good to pass up, but I’d almost rather sit in The Hall of Presidents and watch animatronics talk to me instead of buckling into a vestibular torture chamber.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Aside from a bad case of ride-induced nausea, I think we had a fun and memorable day.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;another-birthday&quot;&gt;Another birthday&lt;a href=&quot;#another-birthday&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;Another birthday&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;Another birthday&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I turned 46 at the end of April. Huzzah. Another year on this rock, orbiting a star, in the arm of a spiral galaxy with a massive black hole in its center. Birthdays of course lose their sentimentality after you’ve had enough. But I had a nice relaxing day, not too many tickets on my desk, with some gloomy weather to preserve a chill mood.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;coming-up&quot;&gt;Coming up&lt;a href=&quot;#coming-up&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;Coming up&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;Coming up&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We’re barreling towards the end of the school year. I’m going back to Redmond again. Then to South Dakota to see my nephew graduate. Then my son will play his 80’s rock show. We got grandparents coming into town. A busy month for sure. Then summer officially begins.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;stats&quot;&gt;Stats&lt;a href=&quot;#stats&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;Stats&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;Stats&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ok, perverts. Here’s the numbers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;-health-and-wellness&quot;&gt;🧠 Health and wellness&lt;a href=&quot;#-health-and-wellness&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;🧠 Health and wellness&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;🧠 Health and wellness&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Still working on untying the knot of ADHD, anxiety, and weight in my life. I feel like I’ve done well on managing the ADHD. The weight could be better (considering how much I’m spending on GLP-1) but I am seeing progress. The anxiety though is hard to shake.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I watched some  &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/@HealthyGamerGG&quot;&gt;Dr. K videos&lt;/a&gt; about &lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/XW-02QiiHDM&quot;&gt;engineer burnout&lt;/a&gt; that resonated with me and another one about &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bj_PSYBCUsM&quot;&gt;ADHD and anxiety&lt;/a&gt; where your brain reinforces itself into a state where you feel like there’s always a tiger in the bushes. That’s how I feel. No acute anxiety, but ambient.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anyways, I’m under doctor’s orders to power down devices at 10:30pm and go to bed. It’s 11:52pm right now. I’ve also resumed going to therapy again (after a bad experience) and hope it sticks this time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3 id=&quot;reading&quot;&gt;📖 Reading&lt;a href=&quot;#reading&quot; aria-label=&quot;Link to heading &apos;📖 Reading&apos;&quot; data-heading-content=&quot;📖 Reading&quot; class=&quot;anchor&quot;&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
